# Security, Tenancy & Privacy

## Tenant isolation
Hierarchy is Platform -> Agency -> Customer -> Agent.
- Customer has exactly one Agency.
- Every protected query and command must enforce ownership server-side.
- Never trust tenant IDs, agency IDs, customer IDs, role claims, permissions or resource IDs supplied by the browser.
- Never use UI filtering as authorization.
- Foreign resource access must not disclose whether the object exists.
- Add explicit cross-tenant negative tests for every tenant-owned endpoint/use case.

## Identity and roles
Platform staff are separate platform users. Tenant users belong to one tenant. Use granular RBAC with distinct platform, agency and customer permission namespaces.
Sensitive capabilities must be explicit: KYC review, KYC document access, payout approval, wallet adjustment, commission changes, suspension, overrides, impersonation and secret access.

## Secrets
- No secrets in source, frontend bundles, fixtures, screenshots, test snapshots or logs.
- Encrypt/secret-manage provider credentials, OAuth refresh tokens and webhook signing secrets.
- Never expose secrets through serializers, admin resources, debugging endpoints or agent prompts.
- Redact secrets from logs and error tracking.

## Web security
- HTTPS/TLS in production.
- Secure HttpOnly/SameSite cookies or equivalent secure sessions.
- CSRF protection for browser state-changing requests where applicable.
- Rate-limit login, OTP/password reset, payout, webhook replay, uploads and expensive endpoints.
- Validate and normalize URLs, phone numbers, uploaded files, webhook payloads and provider responses.
- Prevent SSRF for user-supplied URLs; use explicit scheme/host policy and egress controls.

## KYC and sensitive documents
KYC files are restricted to authorized platform roles and must be segregated from ordinary support/customer views. Preserve version/audit history. Audit sensitive access where feasible.
Never expose private payout proof to agencies. Agency-visible payout receipts contain only non-sensitive metadata.

## Payment data
Use processor tokenization/hosted fields where possible. Never accept/store CVV/CVC or full card authentication data. Payment-card verification images may expose only the final four digits; reject non-compliant uploads.

## Privacy
Apply data minimization, configurable retention, consent/disclosure controls for recording, authorized data export/deletion workflows and legal-retention exceptions. Never claim a jurisdiction is compliant without explicit legal/compliance approval.
